WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.310
From hearing loss to spinal injuries to painful
stunts gone wrong , some actors have suffered

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:10.099
lasting consequences for the sake of making
their art.

00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:14.679
In 2016, production on the final chapter in
the "Maze Runner" series was suspended indefinitely

00:00:14.679 --> 00:00:18.140
due to a horrific on-set accident involving
Dylan O'Brien.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:23.099
The franchise's young star sustained severe
brain trauma when a stunt involving a motorcycle

00:00:23.099 --> 00:00:24.230
went very wrong.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:28.710
O'Brien effectively needed to have his face
put back together by a skilled reconstructive

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:29.710
surgeon.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.039
The actor told The Salt Lake Tribune —

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:33.720
"[I have] four plates that will be a part
of me forever.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:37.420
I feel really lucky to have come away from
it — in terms of coming away from it at

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:40.330
all on the day, and really how my face has
healed.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:44.660
I was really lucky with a really brilliant
doctor, and he salvaged a lot of what I used

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:45.660
to look like.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.130
[...] I think it's really astonishing how
well everything healed and how my doctors

00:00:49.130 --> 00:00:50.379
fixed me up."

00:00:50.379 --> 00:00:54.690
After a bit of a break to heal up, O'Brien
got back to work in Hollywood, but his traumatic

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:57.219
experience left him with some lasting worries.

00:00:57.219 --> 00:00:58.920
In 2020, he told Variety —

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:03.340
"Even to this day, if I’m on set and I’m
doing a stunt, if I’m in a rig, if there’s

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:05.970
some action going on, I am slightly irritable.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.500
There is a degree of anxiety in me that I
don’t think there’s ever not going to

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:10.580
be."

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:15.130
Natalie Portman put herself on a strict diet
and lost 20 pounds to play the part of fragile

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:19.640
ballerina Nina Sayers in Darren Aronofsky's
dark drama "Black Swan."

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.540
She wasn't the only one to take a method approach
to her character, though: her co-star Mila

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.670
Kunis followed suit, but didn't get nearly
as much attention for her transformation.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.820
She told radio host Howard Stern —

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:34.170
"I used to be a smoker, and so I smoked a
lot of cigarettes and I ate a limited amount

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:35.170
of calories.

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:36.930
Twelve hundred calories, and I smoked.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.640
I don't advocate this at all.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:39.640
It was awful."

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.990
Kunis later revealed that her body has never
returned to the state it was in before preparations

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:45.130
for "Black Swan" began.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:46.490
She told Harper's Bazaar —

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:47.500
"My shape is different.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:52.600
When I got down to 95 pounds, I was muscles,
like a little brick house, but skin and bones.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.360
When I gained it back, it went to completely
different areas."

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.210
"I will never dance again.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:01.310
I promise you, I will never put on that pointe
shoe ever again."

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:07.040
In a candid 2018 interview with The New York
Times, Uma Thurman shared some revelations

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.630
about director Quentin Tarantino.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:14.020
During the production of "Kill Bill," Tarantino
reportedly forced Thurman to perform a dangerous

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:16.750
driving scene that left her with lasting damage.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:17.750
She told the Times —

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:18.750
"I was scared.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.920
He said: 'I promise you the car is fine.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.459
It's a straight piece of road.

00:02:22.459 --> 00:02:26.409
Hit 40 miles per hour or your hair won't blow
the right way and I'll make you do it again.'

00:02:26.409 --> 00:02:28.640
[...] But that was a deathbox that I was in.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.560
The seat wasn't screwed down properly.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:33.099
It was a sand road and it was not a straight
road."

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:37.319
Thurman lost control of the Karmann Ghia and
it veered off the road, crashing into a tree

00:02:37.319 --> 00:02:42.120
and leaving her in so much pain that she feared
that she'd lost her ability to walk for good.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:46.010
While it wasn't as bad as she'd initially
feared, the accident left her with lasting

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:47.709
injury to her neck and knees.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:52.400
The accident is part of the reason that Thurman,
who'd also starred in Tarantino's "Pulp Fiction,"

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.060
hasn't worked with the director since "Kill
Bill: Volume 2."

00:02:56.060 --> 00:03:00.610
Wolverine, the adamantium-claw wielding member
of the super-heroically mutated X-Men, is

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:04.069
one of the most pronounced and famous grumps
in all of popular culture.

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:07.970
Every iteration of the character, in print,
on the small screen, and in a series of feature

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:13.380
films spanning two decades, has been depicted
as a scowling, grumbling, tough guy who alternately

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.830
mutters dialogue in a raspy mutter or angry
shout.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.600
No one has played Wolverine more often than
Hugh Jackman, who has strapped on the claws

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:27.159
and taken on that distinctive voice in nine
"X-Men" universe movies between 2000 and 2016.

00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:31.290
Seven years after last playing the role, Jackman
revealed that his voice and vocal cords still

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:34.230
suffer from the consequences of that iconic
voice.

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.440
He told BBC Radio 4's "Front Row" in 2023
—

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.700
"My teacher at drama school would have been
horrified by some of the things I did in 'Wolverine.'

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.450
I've done some damage to my voice, through
playing Wolverine."

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:48.709
Specifically, Jackman has said that when he
performs in musicals, he finds that he can't

00:03:48.709 --> 00:03:50.540
reach the high notes the way he once did.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:51.540
He continued —

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:58.250
"I put that down directly to some of the growling
and yelling I did."

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:03.569
The fishiest star of the DC Universe may look
like an unbeatable, impossible-to-hurt superhero,

00:04:03.569 --> 00:04:05.770
but Jason Momoa is only human.

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:10.750
After the first "Aquaman" movie became a global
box office sensation in 2018, Warner Bros.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.769
quickly ordered a sequel, "Aquaman and the
Lost Kingdom."

00:04:13.769 --> 00:04:18.230
Throughout the filming of the physically demanding
and stunt-heavy action movie, Momoa suffered

00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:21.650
for his art, chalking up his vulnerability
to his advancing age.

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:23.199
The actor told Ellen DeGeneres —

00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:24.199
"I'm getting old.

00:04:24.199 --> 00:04:25.199
I messed up my eyes.

00:04:25.199 --> 00:04:29.729
I just got something in it that kinda cut
it up, and then I've gotta get surgery, I

00:04:29.729 --> 00:04:31.690
have a hernia, I've got ribs out.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.070
I'm just getting beat up."

00:04:33.070 --> 00:04:37.970
In addition to what he confirmed was a scratched
cornea and hernia, Momoa revealed to Yahoo!

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:42.810
that he wound up with a facial scar after
a fight scene in the 2018 movie "Braven" went

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:43.810
sideways.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:44.810
He recalled —

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:45.810
"I got knocked out.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:46.810
A stunt guy hit me.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:47.810
He blindsided me.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:51.139
We didn't make eye contact, and he hit me
before I could grab for him, and everything

00:04:51.139 --> 00:04:52.139
went black."

00:04:52.139 --> 00:04:56.740
Momoa required hospitalization for the incident,
and shooting on "Braven" was put on hold for

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:58.330
a day.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.910
After breaking into stardom with her role
as Van on FX's award-winning dramedy "Atlanta,"

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:06.760
Zazie Beetz was suddenly being offered work
in movies much bigger than the shorts and

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:08.690
indies in which she was used to performing.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:14.270
In 2018, she co-starred in the big-budget
superhero comedy "Deadpool 2" as Domino, a

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:17.120
member of the titular hero's X-Force of powerful
rogues.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:18.440
Domino's special power?

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:19.919
She's extremely lucky.

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:21.641
"Luck isn't a superpower."

00:05:21.641 --> 00:05:22.590
"Yes it is."

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:23.192
"No it isn't."

00:05:23.192 --> 00:05:23.692
"Yes it is."

00:05:23.692 --> 00:05:24.328
"No ma'am!"

00:05:24.328 --> 00:05:24.828
"Mm-hmm."

00:05:24.828 --> 00:05:25.828
"Mario No-pez."

00:05:25.828 --> 00:05:26.828
"Yeah."

00:05:26.828 --> 00:05:30.050
Life did not imitate art on the set of "Deadpool
2," unfortunately.

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:34.770
According to USA Today, the film was a physically
grueling experience for the actors, with Beetz

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:37.730
training for four hours a day for months before
filming began.

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:41.389
That was far from the worst part, though,
as things would continue to go wrong for her

00:05:41.389 --> 00:05:42.920
once rehearsal ended.

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:46.779
During one action sequence that involved a
high-speed truck chase, Beetz lost consciousness

00:05:46.779 --> 00:05:51.300
— and during an elaborate fight scene, a
stray casing from a freshly fired shell landed

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.419
on her chest, burning her skin and leaving
a permanent scar.

00:05:55.419 --> 00:05:59.830
Alfre Woodard agreed to take on the role of
21st-century spacecraft builder Lily Sloane

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:04.590
in the 1996 film "Star Trek: First Contact"
because she was friendly with several members

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:10.050
of the cast, including Brent Spiner, LeVar
Burton, and especially director Jonathan Frakes.

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:14.150
A pivotal scene called for a massive explosion
when the enemy Borg sends a ship into the

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:17.900
past, which slams into Earth and sends many
of the good guys flying.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.810
Woodard recalls practicing the sequence for
many hours, in which she and a number of stunt

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:25.690
performers were to jump as if they'd been
knocked into the air, spin around, and land

00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:27.490
so the cameras could capture their reactions.

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:28.490
Woodard told Collider —

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:31.819
"The explosion was so intense it just knocked
us off our butts.

00:06:31.819 --> 00:06:33.229
We just fell any way we could."

00:06:33.229 --> 00:06:37.546
At that point, Woodard realized she'd torn
up her costume and her arms were covered in blood.

00:06:37.546 --> 00:06:42.099
Frakes got his shot, but Woodard still bears
scars on both of her arms from the stunt.

00:06:42.099 --> 00:06:46.500
A lot can go wrong on a movie set, even on
a relatively low-tech comedy.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.340
Case in point: in 1986, Steve Martin, along
with Martin Short and Chevy Chase, starred

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:53.490
in the action-tinged comedy "Three Amigos!"

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:58.069
In this goofy romp, three silent-era Western
film stars get stuck in a terrorized Mexican

00:06:58.069 --> 00:07:01.590
village and have to save its residents who
think they are the brave heroes they play

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:05.370
in the movies — and not the spoiled and
cowardly actors they really are.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:09.180
Martin's character, Lucky Day, deals with
a number of uncomfortable situations to which

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:12.200
he's unsuited, including several shootouts.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:16.419
That may have led to a case of tinnitus — a
persistent, high-pitched, and grating ringing

00:07:16.419 --> 00:07:17.419
of the ears.

00:07:17.419 --> 00:07:21.389
The actor and comedian has suffered from the
condition ever since the production of "Three

00:07:21.389 --> 00:07:23.650
Amigos!," but he thinks something else could've
also triggered it.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:25.289
He told Pitchfork in 2015 —

00:07:25.289 --> 00:07:28.250
"I was shooting The Three Amigos and I got
tinnitus.

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:32.760
I blamed it on gunfire at the time but since
then I realized it was just my destiny after

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:37.210
listening to music too loud my whole life
and playing live concerts with 28,000 screaming

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:39.800
people — it was like being in a football
stadium."

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.840
"It's like living with a six-year-old."

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:45.159
Thankfully, Martin has learned to live with
the tinnitus.

00:07:45.159 --> 00:07:46.159
He continued —

00:07:46.159 --> 00:07:48.789
"It becomes your natural background noise."

00:07:48.789 --> 00:07:53.070
While making 2013's "Thor: The Dark World,"
Jaimie Alexander — who plays Thor's ally

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:55.940
Sif — suffered a painful-sounding injury.

00:07:55.940 --> 00:08:00.349
Speaking with MTV News, Alexander explained
that she'd slipped and fallen from an unfortunate

00:08:00.349 --> 00:08:01.569
height, elaborating —

00:08:01.569 --> 00:08:06.610
"I herniated a disk in my thoracic spine,
dislocated my left shoulder, tore my right

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.889
rhomboid, and chipped 11 vertebrae."

00:08:08.889 --> 00:08:12.970
Alexander thought the injury might not be
a big deal, but the next morning, it was still

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:14.949
pretty bad — and then it got worse.

00:08:14.949 --> 00:08:15.949
She continued —

00:08:15.949 --> 00:08:19.500
"I got in a car to go to the hospital, and
I sat in the car and compressed my spine a

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:23.150
little bit, and went paralyzed in my right
leg and my right hand."

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:28.289
After a month of physical therapy, Alexander
was back on set swinging a sword — but spinal

00:08:28.289 --> 00:08:30.850
injuries like that don't just go away entirely.

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:34.669
It seems like playing an Asgardian might not
be all it's cracked up to be.

00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:39.690
Blake Lively's 2016 hit "The Shallows" proved
that she had some genuine action chops — and

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:43.610
the actor was duly rewarded with a lead role
in her very own spy thriller, "The Rhythm

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:44.610
Section."

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:49.580
Sadly, things went south for Lively in late
2017, as an on-set injury she sustained while

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:53.519
filming an action sequence with Jude Law caused
the film to halt production.

00:08:53.519 --> 00:08:57.270
Sources informed The Hollywood Reporter that
Lively's hand injury wasn't thought to be

00:08:57.270 --> 00:09:01.820
all that serious at first, but the surgery
she underwent after sustaining it apparently

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:03.420
failed to fix the issue.

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:07.930
Further surgeries followed, with both Paramount
and Lively awaiting instruction from surgeons

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:09.330
on how best to proceed.

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:12.700
In early 2018, she was cleared to return to
the set.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:16.760
Filming resumed later in the year, but Lively's
hand never quite returned back to the way

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:17.760
it was before.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.209
"I severed a ligament here, so my knuckles
are kinda dropped, and I, you know, dislocated

00:09:22.209 --> 00:09:23.940
these two bones…"

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:29.320
In "The Wizard of Oz," the beloved 1939 film
adaptation of L. Frank Baum's fantasy novel,

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:33.250
Jack Haley played the Tin Man, the friendly
metal monster on his way to see the Wizard

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:34.550
to acquire a heart.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:39.220
However, Haley wasn't MGM's first choice for
the role — or even the first actor cast.

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:43.660
According to Vanity Fair, Buddy Ebsen — best
known for his later leading gigs on "The Beverly

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:46.740
Hillbillies" and "Barnaby Jones" — was hired
first.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:50.540
When rehearsing in makeup and under studio
lights, the crew repeatedly covered Ebsen's

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:54.279
face with aluminum dust to keep his white
grease paint from running.

00:09:54.279 --> 00:09:58.380
One night during the rehearsal period, Ebsen
awoke with painful cramps in his limbs.

00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:00.640
His face had turned blue, and he couldn't
breathe.

00:10:00.640 --> 00:10:04.980
The actor spent two weeks of hospitalization
in an oxygen tent — his body had to divest

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:08.580
itself of the large amount of toxic aluminum
sitting in his lungs.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:13.674
With his recovery taking longer than they
liked, MGM fired Ebsen and installed Haley instead.

00:10:13.674 --> 00:10:18.080
Another cast member of "The Wizard of Oz"
also experienced a severe medical reaction.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:22.130
Ray Bolger portrayed the brainless Scarecrow,
and on the final day of shooting, he took

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:25.740
off his rubber-and burlap-based prosthetic
mask for the last time.

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:29.740
When he did, he discovered that he had received
burlap-like scars all over his chin and mouth.

